---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import '../styles/landing.css';

const mcpConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "loomweaver": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@loomweaver/mcp"] }
  }
}`;

const commandExample = `ctx.registerCommand({
  id: 'invoices.export',
  title: 'invoices.export',
  description: 'Export the selected invoices as CSV',
  arguments: [
    { name: 'range', kind: 'choice', choices: ['month', 'quarter', 'year'], required: true },
  ],
  callable: true,
  run: (_context, args) => exportInvoices(String(args?.['range'])),
});`;

const quickStart = `# a fresh Angular app, or the one you already have
ng new my-studio --style=css --ssr=false && cd my-studio

# the platform
npm install @loomweaver/shell @loomweaver/plugin-sdk @angular/cdk @jsverse/transloco \\
  @ng-icons/heroicons @angular/service-worker
npm install -D tailwindcss @tailwindcss/postcss @tailwindcss/typography

# your product, and a first plugin already contributing to it
npx @loomweaver/cli distribution --name my-studio --title "My Studio" --out . --force
npx @loomweaver/cli weaver --id notes --command --shortcut 'mod+shift+n' --out src/notes

ng serve`;

const included = [
  'Tab groups with drag-to-split panes, and any tab pops out into its own window',
  'A command palette (⌘K) and quick open (⌘P), with preview tabs and pinning',
  'Named workspaces that remember their own arrangement',
  'Theming from semantic tokens: bring Tailwind, bring Bootstrap, bring nothing',
  'i18n with namespaced composition, so two plugins can never collide on a key',
  'WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility the host meets and your plugins inherit',
  'An installable PWA with an update flow that actually works',
  'Live state sync across windows and tabs',
  'Save, discard or cancel when an editor closes with unsaved work',
  'Auth-aware chrome: contributions declare the access they need, the shell reacts',
];

const rungs = [
  {
    name: 'Trusted, in-process',
    body: 'Your own weavers, composed at build time. Full reach, because you wrote them.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Sandboxed iframe',
    body: 'Somebody else’s code in its own JS context, opaque origin, no reach into your DOM. Write the body in React, Vue, Svelte or plain JS.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Installed at runtime',
    body: 'From your curated catalog, with a consent dialog the user answers and an update path driven by the catalog version.',
  },
];
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'LoomWeaver',
    description:
      'The workbench your product runs in: panes, tabs, a command palette, theming and a plugin store, ready on day one. Your domain arrives as a plugin, and so does everyone else’s.',
    template: 'splash',
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
  hasSidebar={false}
>
  <div class="lw-landing">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Apache 2.0 · seven npm packages · ships no server</p>
      <h1>Build the product.<br />Not the workbench.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Panes the user can split, tabs that pop out into their own window, a command palette,
        theming, i18n and a plugin store. All of it there on day one. Your domain arrives as
        a plugin. So does everyone else’s.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/getting-started/">Start in 5 minutes</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/architecture/">See the architecture</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/yesbert/loomweaver">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <figure class="shot tour">
      <video
        autoplay
        muted
        loop
        playsinline
        controls
        preload="metadata"
        poster="/media/tour-poster.jpg"
        width="1280"
        height="800"
        aria-label="A tour of a LoomWeaver workbench: opening the command palette, splitting a pane, a sandboxed non-Angular plugin, and a plugin re-skinning the whole application"
      >
        <source src="/media/tour.webm" type="video/webm" />
        <source src="/media/tour.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
      <figcaption>
        Twenty-six seconds, no cuts. The rail, the panes, the palette and the status bar are the
        platform’s. Everything inside them comes from plugins, including the theme at the end.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <script is:inline>
      const tour = document.querySelector('.tour video');
      if (tour && matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
        tour.autoplay = false;
        tour.pause();
      }
    </script>

    <section class="doors">
      <h2>Two reasons people pick this up</h2>
      <div class="door-grid">
        <article class="door">
          <p class="eyebrow">If you build with an AI assistant</p>
          <h3>It learns this UI once, then it knows every project you start.</h3>
          <p>
            Right now every new project means teaching it your structure, your routing and your
            conventions all over again. You write another instructions file, and it still invents
            things. The UI is exactly where it invents most.
          </p>
          <p>
            LoomWeaver is the same workbench every time, and it is written down for machines:
            <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> as the map, <a href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a> as
            the whole contract in a single fetch, and <code>@loomweaver/mcp</code> so your assistant
            scaffolds a weaver with tools instead of guesses.
          </p>
          <Code code={mcpConfig} lang="json" title=".mcp.json" />
          <p class="aside">
            Then just ask for it: “add a weaver called invoices with a command and a settings
            section”.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="door">
          <p class="eyebrow">If you want your users to extend it</p>
          <h3>A plugin system is not a feature. It is half a year. This one is already here.</h3>
          <p>
            You built a good tool. People have ideas, and some of them would build those ideas
            themselves if they could. They can’t, because there is no way in, and opening one means
            isolation, permissions, a store, updates and an API you promise not to break.
          </p>
          <p>
            All of that is here. And there is no privileged host API: your own product UI goes
            through the exact same door a stranger’s plugin does, which is the only reason a
            published contract does not quietly rot. Your community can do everything you can do.
          </p>
          <p class="aside">
            Every tool with a living ecosystem got there the same way. The plugins made the
            product, not the roadmap.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="bridge">
        And the two feed each other. Somebody who wants to contribute to <em>your</em> tool points
        their own assistant at your <code>llms-full.txt</code> and starts.
        <strong>Your contributor onboarding is a URL.</strong>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="feature">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Register the action once. It is a button, a shortcut, a palette entry and an agent tool.</h2>
        <p>
          The rail item, the keystroke, the context menu and the command palette already point at the
          same command. <code>callable: true</code> adds one more caller: an agent, through
          <code>@loomweaver/ag-ui</code>. You never keep a second list of tools beside the first, and
          the guarantee holds without you writing a line of it.
        </p>
        <p class="rule">An agent reaches what the user could have reached, and nothing more.</p>
        <p>
          <a href="/reference/callable-commands/">Callable commands</a> ·
          <a href="/reference/agent-tools/">Agent tools</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Code code={commandExample} lang="ts" title="your weaver" />
    </section>

    <figure class="shot narrow">
      <img
        src="/media/command-palette.png"
        alt="The LoomWeaver command palette open over a dashboard, listing commands contributed by plugins"
        width="1880"
        height="1400"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Every command in the palette was contributed by a plugin. So was the dashboard behind it.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="checklist">
      <h2>What you would otherwise build twice</h2>
      <ul>
        {included.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="rungs">
      <h2>Three rungs of trust, not one switch</h2>
      <div class="rung-grid">
        {
          rungs.map((rung, index) => (
            <article class="rung">
              <span class="num">{index + 1}</span>
              <h3>{rung.name}</h3>
              <p>{rung.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="bridge">
        All three consume the same <code>ctx</code>, behind a default-deny capability broker the user
        can inspect and revoke. Moving a plugin down a rung is a change of trust, not a rewrite.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="feature reverse">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2>Frontend only, on purpose</h2>
        <p>
          LoomWeaver ships no server. Settings, working state and auth are frontend ports with local
          defaults, so the UI runs standalone from the first minute and you wire it to your own
          backend, in any stack, when you are ready.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing about your domain lives in the core, and nothing ever can. A product is a
          distribution that composes the published packages. It never forks anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/backend-integration/">Backend integration</a> ·
          <a href="/building-a-distribution/">Building a distribution</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Code code={quickStart} lang="bash" frame="terminal" />
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
      <h2>Five minutes to a running product</h2>
      <p>
        The scaffold wires the build, the styles, the service worker and the content-security policy,
        registers your first weaver in the composition root, and names every file it touched. It only
        ever adds.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/getting-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/samples/">Copyable recipes</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="https://demo.loomweaver.dev">Live demo</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</StarlightPage>
